# Task 1

This task focuses on implementing the LASSO (Least Absolute Shrinkage and Selection Operator) regression algorithm using the shooting method, a coordinate descent technique. The goal is to create custom R functions for estimating LASSO coefficients, evaluating performance over a range of penalty parameters (λ), and conducting model selection through 10-fold cross-validation. These custom implementations are then compared with the widely-used glmnet package to ensure correctness and efficiency. Simulated data is used to validate the methods and to demonstrate their ability to identify optimal regularization parameters by minimizing Mean Squared Error (MSE) and Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE).

### 1.1 Write your own function for the lasso using the shooting algorithm. Give default values for the tolerance limit and the maximum number of iterations. Do not forget to compute the coefficient for the intercept.

This task involves implementing the LASSO regression algorithm using the shooting method, a coordinate descent technique. The goal is to create a custom function that iteratively updates the coefficients to minimize the LASSO objective function. The implementation should include default values for convergence tolerance and maximum iterations, ensuring flexibility and efficiency. Additionally, the function must account for the intercept term, which plays a crucial role in centering the data and improving model accuracy. This foundational implementation serves as the basis for further analysis and comparison in subsequent tasks.

``` {r}
shooting_lasso <- function(X, y, lambda, tol = 1e-6, max_iter = 1000) {
  if (!is.matrix(X)) X <- as.matrix(X)
  if (!is.numeric(y) || length(y) != nrow(X)) stop("y must be numeric and match the number of rows in X.")
  if (!is.numeric(lambda) || lambda < 0) stop("lambda must be a non-negative numeric value.")
  
  n <- nrow(X)
  p <- ncol(X)
  beta <- rep(0, p)
  intercept <- mean(y)
  y_centered <- y - intercept
  X_centered <- scale(X, center = TRUE, scale = FALSE)
  z_j <- colSums(X_centered^2)
  
  for (iter in 1:max_iter) {
    beta_old <- beta
    for (j in 1:p) {
      r_j <- y_centered - X_centered[, -j, drop = FALSE] %*% beta[-j]
      rho_j <- sum(X_centered[, j] * r_j)
      beta[j] <- sign(rho_j) * max(0, abs(rho_j) - lambda) / z_j[j]
    }
    if (sqrt(sum((beta - beta_old)^2)) < tol) break
  }
  
  if (iter == max_iter) warning("Algorithm did not converge within max_iter.")
  return(list(intercept = intercept, coefficients = beta))
}
```

The code above implements the function.

