### 1.2 Write a function which computes the lasso using your algorithm for a vector of λs and which returns the matrix of coefficients and the corresponding λ values.

In this task, we extend the LASSO implementation by modifying the function to handle a vector of regularization parameters (λ) and compute the corresponding coefficients for each value of λ. The function will iterate over the provided λ values, applying the shooting algorithm to estimate the coefficients for each, and then return a matrix of coefficients alongside the associatedλ values. This approach allows for a comprehensive exploration of how the LASSO coefficients change with different levels of regularization, aiding in model selection and tuning.

``` {r}
lasso_path <- function(X, y, lambdas, tol = 1e-6, max_iter = 1000) {
  if (!is.matrix(X)) stop("X must be a matrix.")
  if (!is.numeric(y) || length(y) != nrow(X)) stop("y must be numeric and match the number of rows in X.")
  if (!is.numeric(lambdas) || length(lambdas) == 0) stop("lambdas must be a numeric vector.")
  
  p <- ncol(X)
  coef_matrix <- matrix(0, nrow = length(lambdas), ncol = p + 1)
  colnames(coef_matrix) <- c("Intercept", paste0("V", 1:p))
  
  for (i in 1:length(lambdas)) {
    lambda <- lambdas[i]
    fit <- shooting_lasso(X, y, lambda, tol, max_iter)
    coef_matrix[i, ] <- c(fit$intercept, fit$coefficients)
  }
  
  return(data.frame(lambda = lambdas, coef_matrix))
}
```

The code above implements the function.

