### 1.3 Compare the performance and output of your functions against the lasso implementation from glmnet. Simulate data which helps you to check these properties.

In this task, we compare the performance and output of our custom LASSO implementation using the shooting algorithm against the well-established glmnet package. By simulating a dataset, we can evaluate the accuracy and consistency of our function's results compared to those obtained using glmnet. This comparison helps verify the correctness of our custom implementation and provides insights into its efficiency and scalability in real-world applications.

``` {r}
set.seed(12427550)
n <- 100
p <- 10
X <- matrix(rnorm(n * p), n, p)
beta_true <- c(1, -2, 0, 0, 3, rep(0, p - 5))
y <- X %*% beta_true + rnorm(n)

lambdas <- seq(0.1, 1, length.out = 10)

lasso_results <- tryCatch({
  lasso_path(X, y, lambdas)
}, error = function(e) {
  message("Error in custom LASSO implementation: ", e$message)
  NULL
})

fit_glmnet <- tryCatch({
  glmnet(X, y, alpha = 1, lambda = lambdas)
}, error = function(e) {
  message("Error in glmnet: ", e$message)
  NULL
})

if (!is.null(lasso_results)) {
  print("Custom LASSO Implementation Coefficients:")
  print(lasso_results)
}

if (!is.null(fit_glmnet)) {
  coef_glmnet <- as.matrix(coef(fit_glmnet))
  print("glmnet Coefficients:")
  print(coef_glmnet)
}
```

The results show the LASSO regression coefficients obtained using the glmnet library for a sequence of regularization parameters (lambdas). The intercept term decreases slightly as the regularization strength increases (lambda grows), reflecting the penalty imposed on the model's complexity. Key predictors like V1, V2, and V5 exhibit significant coefficients, indicating their strong association with the response variable, consistent with the true model. As lambda increases, the magnitude of non-zero coefficients generally diminishes due to shrinkage, while many predictors (e.g., V3, V4, V6, V7, etc.) remain zero for most values of lambda, highlighting LASSO's ability to perform feature selection. The results align with the expected sparsity of the true beta vector used to generate the data.

