# Task 3

### Explain the notion of regularised regression, shrinkage and how Ridge regression and LASSO regression differ.

Regularized regression is a technique used to improve the generalization of regression models by adding a penalty term to the objective function. This penalty discourages the model from overfitting by shrinking the regression coefficients, which helps control the complexity of the model. The primary goal of regularization is to prevent the model from becoming too sensitive to the training data, especially when there are many features or when the features are highly correlated. The most common regularization methods are Ridge regression and LASSO (Least Absolute Shrinkage and Selection Operator).

In regularized regression, shrinkage refers to the process of forcing the regression coefficients toward zero, effectively reducing the impact of less important features on the model. Ridge regression applies a penalty proportional to the square of the coefficients (L2 norm), which leads to smaller coefficients, but does not necessarily set any coefficients exactly to zero. This allows Ridge to include all features in the model, but with smaller weights for less important ones. In contrast, LASSO regression uses the absolute values of the coefficients (L1 norm) as its penalty. This tends to force some coefficients exactly to zero, effectively performing feature selection. As a result, LASSO can produce simpler, sparser models by excluding irrelevant features, while Ridge regression tends to keep all features in the model with reduced magnitudes.

The key difference between Ridge and LASSO lies in the nature of the penalty. Ridge regression is suited for situations where we believe all features contribute to the model but want to reduce their influence, while LASSO is ideal when we expect that only a subset of the features are truly important and wish to exclude irrelevant ones. In practice, these methods can be combined in Elastic Net, which balances the strengths of both Ridge and LASSO, offering a more flexible approach to regularization.
